ROCclr (gfx7/gfx8), ROCm 6.4.4: the command processor does not maintain the
shader caches at a dispatch boundary. A kernel that reads a page which a copy
kernel refilled a moment earlier can be served the page's previous contents out
of its CU's vector L1. One PM4 ACQUIRE_MEM packet, published in its own AQL
ring slot, invalidates the vector L1 and the scalar cache:

  1. Ahead of every kernel dispatch (the reader direction that fails on 6.4.4).
  2. Inside releaseGpuMemoryFence(), between its barrier and a second barrier.
  3. Ahead of an event-record barrier.

Sites 2 and 3 are written for a copy engine or the host reading data a kernel
has not yet written back. That direction does not fail on 6.4.4: ROCr uses no
SDMA on gfx8 by default and small copies are shader copies that go through L2.
The packet word does not request an L2 writeback, so these two sites add no
coverage for it.

All three sites are gated on `dev().isa().versionMajor() <= 8` and on the new
`CLR_GFX8_TC_INVALIDATE` flag (default on), so one binary measures both ways.
Site 3 has its own flag, `CLR_GFX8_TC_RECORD_FENCE`.

WHY
---
ROCm expresses cross-dispatch coherence only through the AQL packet header's
SCACQUIRE and SCRELEASE fence scopes. rocvirtual.cpp states the contract it
relies on: an agent-scope acquire "invalidates I, K and L1", a system-scope
release "invalidates L1, L2 and flushes L2". The compiler relies on the same
contract: the LLVM AMDGPU memory model for GFX6-GFX9 emits no cache
maintenance at kernel entry because "the scalar and vector caches are
invalidated between kernel dispatches by CP" (AMDGPUUsage.rst, "Memory Model
GFX6-GFX9"). That contract is implemented in MEC firmware, and on gfx803 with
MEC firmware 0x2da it is not honoured for the data caches: nothing in CLR or
ROCr 6.4.4 ever emits TCL1_ACTION_ENA, and the only ACQUIRE_MEM the data path
ever sees is ROCr's code-cache invalidate at code-object load
(GpuAgent::InvalidateCodeCaches).

The reproducer is a direct rocBLAS probe: about 500 small fp32 GEMM, GEMV and
TRSM calls, each one doing hipMalloc, hipMemcpy H2D, the rocBLAS call,
hipMemcpy D2H, hipFree. It fails deterministically at the same call every run
(rocblas_sgemm_strided_batched m=n=k=8 batch 4, relative error 0.94,
bit-identical), and passes all 511 cases when hipFree is a no-op, so the wrong
answer needs a recycled address. The chain that recycles it is entirely in user
space and entirely kernel-written:

  * hipFree waits for every stream on the device before it releases the buffer
    (hip_memory.cpp ihipFree: SyncAllStreams()), so no kernel is in flight when
    the address comes back. This is not a free-before-completion race.
  * ROCr sub-allocates ordinary VRAM below 2 MiB from a fragment heap
    (amd_kfd_driver.cpp KfdDriver::AllocateMemory; simple_heap.h, best fit).
    A freed 4 KiB page goes back on the free list and the next hipMalloc of the
    same size gets the same virtual and the same physical page. No ioctl, no
    unmap, no TLB flush, no VRAM clear is involved.
  * Copies of 16 KiB or less go through CLR's shader copy path
    (rocblit.cpp: totalSize <= sdmaCopyThreshold_, GPU_FORCE_BLIT_COPY_SIZE=16),
    and ROCr does not use SDMA on gfx8 at all by default
    (amd_gpu_agent.cpp: "User SDMA queues are unstable on gfx8"). The new data
    therefore reaches the page through L2, where every CU sees it; only a CU
    whose L1 still holds the page's old lines sees the old bytes.

A full L2 writeback and invalidate is included for the other direction: the
copy engine and the host read memory and never look at the shader caches, so a
D2H copy of a buffer a kernel just wrote copies the pre-write bytes unless the
dirty lines were pushed out first. ROCm 10.x on the same card showed exactly
that (conv output right on the GPU, wrong in the host copy, in 64-float blocks),
and the same slot in the same two places fixed it there.

The CP_COHER_CNTL word requests TCL1_ACTION_ENA (the vector L1) and SH_KCACHE_ACTION_ENA
(the scalar cache): 0x08400000. Measured with one library and a run-time override of the word
(probe_rocblas.py, 511 cases): the vector L1 alone cures the probe, and so do the L1 and
scalar-cache pair and the L2 pair the ROCm 10.x patch used (TC_ACTION_ENA with
TC_WB_ACTION_ENA); the L2 invalidate without the writeback does not cure it, with or
without the L1 bit, and neither does an empty word. Decode speed on a 7B Q4_K model against
the knob switched off: L1 only -1.7% to -2.8%, L1 plus scalar cache -2.3%, the L2 pair
-2.7%, the full amdgpu set (adding TC, TC_WB and SH_ICACHE) -7.3%; prefill is unchanged. The
instruction cache is left out because ROCr invalidates it when a code object loads, and
the L2 pair is left out because shader copies write through L2.

WHAT
----
`rocclr/utils/flags.hpp`: `CLR_GFX8_TC_INVALIDATE` and `CLR_GFX8_TC_RECORD_FENCE`
(both default on).

`rocclr/device/rocm/rocvirtual.{hpp,cpp}`: `VirtualGPU::insertTcFence()` takes
one 64-byte AQL slot with `hsa_queue_add_write_index_screlease`, waits for the
slot to be free the way dispatchGenericAqlPacket does, and fills it with
ACQUIRE_MEM (whole address range, poll interval 0xA), a NOP, and the trailing
RELEASE_MEM whose EVENT_INDEX(AQL) retires the slot. This is the PM4-inside-an-
AQL-slot protocol ROCr's gfx8 `AqlQueue::ExecutePM4()` uses, with the payload
inlined instead of fetched through an indirect buffer. dword 0 is stored last
with release semantics. The slot rings no doorbell: the packet the caller writes
next has a higher ring index and its doorbell commits this slot too. ACQUIRE_MEM
stalls the CP until the cache action is complete, so the next packet cannot
launch ahead of it. Three call sites:

  * `dispatchGenericAqlPacket()`, before the dispatch's ring index is taken,
    for `hsa_kernel_dispatch_packet_t` only. In 6.4.4 the graph replay path
    (`dispatchAqlPacket(uint8_t*, ...)`) also goes through this function one
    packet at a time, so graph dispatches get the slot as well.
  * `releaseGpuMemoryFence()`, between its barrier and a second barrier. The
    writeback must follow the first barrier, which carries the dependency
    signals of producers on other queues, and a barrier must follow the
    writeback, because the following copy waits on the queue's current
    completion signal.
  * `submitMarker()`, ahead of the releasing barrier of an event record. The
    raw slot only: no barrier, no completion signal, so it takes no part in the
    marker's signal bookkeeping.

COST: one 64-byte ring slot plus a full-range vector L1 and scalar cache
invalidate per dispatch, on gfx7 and gfx8 only. Measured decode cost on a 7B Q4_K
model is 2.3% to 2.7% (see above). The ROCm 10.x queue cap of 64 packets does not
apply: ROC_AQL_QUEUE_SIZE is 16384 in 6.4.4.

Applies to: ROCm/clr tag rocm-6.4.4 (commit 123eb5128769ba30fda8f84414421bc858e8c695),
with `patch -p1` from the clr checkout root. Apply with
clr-gfx8-tc-invalidate-6.4.4.sh.

VERIFICATION (RX 590, gfx803, MEC firmware 730, image built with this patch):
  * probe_rocblas.py, 511 cases: all pass with the flag on; with
    CLR_GFX8_TC_INVALIDATE=0 on the same binary the run stops at case 225
    (rocblas_sgemm_strided_batched m=n=k=8 batch 4, relative error 0.94).
  * torch.linalg through rocSOLVER (tools/correctness-suite/pytorch/linalg_check.py):
    204 of 204 cases up to n=257; before the patch the same run returned wrong
    factorizations and, at n=64 to 129, a memory access fault.
  * The correctness suite regression set (shim bit identity, conv checks, UNet
    check, rocPRIM and top-k ops, 20 training steps) passes with no GPU fault.
  * tools/tc-staleness: probe.py, soak.py, graphprobe.py, conbrace3.py and ms3.py
    are clean with the flag on. They are also clean with it off on this stack, so
    they do not isolate this bug; probe_rocblas.py does.
  Not covered: the L2 writeback direction (no failure reproduces on 6.4.4), why the
  L1 plus L2-invalidate word without writeback fails while the L1 word alone and
  the L2 pair pass, and workloads other than the ones above.

